  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component is fundamentally inaccessible to keyboard and screen reader users because it relies on a non-interactive `<div>` for its primary trigger mechanism. It completely lacks the ARIA attributes required by the WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern to communicate state and relationships to assistive technology. This design must be revised before implementation.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1. **CRITICAL**: Using a `<div>` instead of `<button>` for an interactive trigger will break keyboard operability and screen reader announcement.\n2. **CRITICAL**: Missing `aria-expanded` means the toggle state is invisible to assistive technology.\n3. **MAJOR**: The `+`/`\u2212` visual indicators are exposed to screen readers, causing redundant or confusing announcements.\n4. **MAJOR**: No relationship established between the trigger and the content panel (`aria-controls`).\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n\n1. **Non-interactive element used as a trigger**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` \u2014 `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern requires a native `<button>` element for the trigger.\n   - **Fix**: Replace the `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with a `<button type=\"button\" className=\"expandable-trigger\">`. This provides native keyboard focusability and activation (Enter/Space) and announces itself as a button.\n\n2. **Missing `aria-expanded` state on trigger**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` \u2014 The trigger has no ARIA attributes communicating its state.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern (Trigger). The trigger must have `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to communicate the current state of the panel.\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the `<button>` element.\n\n3. **Missing relationship between trigger and content**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` (trigger) and `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:16` (content panel). No IDs or references exist.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users, Cognitive accessibility users.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern. The trigger must reference the panel via `aria-controls`, and the panel should ideally be a landmark region.\n   - **Fix**: \n     1. Add a unique `id` to the content panel: `<div id={`content-${title}`} className=\"expandable-content\">`.\n     2. Add `aria-controls={id}` to the trigger button.\n     3. Add `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` to the content panel for better grouping.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n\n1. **Visual state indicators exposed to assistive technology**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:12` \u2014 `{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). Symbols like `+` and `\u2212` are announced as \"plus\" or \"minus\". When combined with the button role and state, this creates noisy announcements (e.g., \"Expand section, button, expanded, minus\").\n   - **Fix**: Wrap the symbols in a span with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`: `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n2. **Content panel lacks semantic role**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:16` \u2014 `<div className=\"expandable-content\">`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern. The content panel should be marked as a region to help screen reader users understand it is a distinct, toggleable block of content.\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` pointing back to the trigger button to the content `<div>`.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n\n1. **Missing accessible name for the region (if title is purely visual)**\n   - If the `title` prop is only used visually, the `role=\"region\"` on the content panel needs an explicit `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` that describes the section, not just the trigger text, to ensure context when navigating by landmarks.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Native Semantic HTML**: The component uses a `<div>` for interactivity instead of a native `<button>`. ARIA cannot fix a missing semantic element; it must be replaced with the correct HTML tag.\n- **Keyboard Operability**: No `onKeyDown` handler is present (though unnecessary if using `<button>`), and no focus management is designed because the trigger isn't focusable.\n- **State Communication**: The component relies entirely on visual cues (`+`/`\u2212`) for state, which fails users who cannot see the UI.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears \"title plus\" (or minus). Does not know it is clickable. When they try to navigate by headings or landmarks, the content panel is invisible because it lacks `role=\"region\"` and proper association.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Tabbing through the page skips over the trigger entirely because `<div>` elements are not focusable by default. The component is completely unusable.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom)**: If the visual styling relies on color or small hit areas, it may be difficult to discern clickability without a clear focus ring (which won't appear since the element isn't focusable).\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: The interaction model is ambiguous for users who rely on assistive technology because the state change is not announced programmatically.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: \nThe component fails at the most fundamental level of web accessibility: it uses non-semantic HTML for interactive content. This breaks keyboard operability (WCAG 2.1.1) and prevents screen readers from understanding the widget's role or state (WCAG 4.1.2). It cannot be accepted in its current form. The design must be revised to use a native `<button>` and implement the complete WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the `title` prop always contain unique text? If multiple instances of this component are used, the `id` generation logic (e.g., `content-${title}`) must ensure uniqueness to avoid ARIA ID collisions.",
